# Observability

What this project reports about itself, so you can see what it did: log lines
on every request, and whatever the options below send. Kafka and domain
events (`OrderPlaced`) are a different thing, not covered here.

## Request IDs

Every HTTP request gets an ID (`core/request_id.py`). A caller may send its own
in `X-Request-ID` (letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `-`, up to 128 characters), so one
ID follows a call from the mobile app through this API; anything else is
replaced with a new one. The response always sends the ID back, and it is on
every log line the request writes, including those of the background
tasks it sends when the project has a worker.

When a user reports a problem, ask for the ID shown in the app (or read it from
the response), then search the logs for it.

## Logs

Write logs with the standard library, one logger per file, and put the values
worth searching for in `extra=` rather than inside the message:

```python
logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)

logger.info("Order placed", extra={"order_id": order.id})
```

`LOG_FORMAT` (in `.env`) decides how lines are written:

- `text`, the default: `2026-11-27 10:00:00,000 INFO     [3f2a…] shop.orders: Order placed`
- `json`, which docker-compose sets: one JSON object per line, with
  `time`, `level`, `logger`, `message`, `request_id` and every `extra=` field,
  ready for Loki, CloudWatch or Datadog.

Each request also writes one line of its own, from the
`{{ name.package }}.access` logger: method, path, status and duration in
milliseconds. `/health` and `/metrics` are left out; probes would bury real traffic.

Never log passwords, tokens or personal details: log lines are kept for a long
time and read by many people.

